# Performance Optimization Analysis for Ruflo/Claude-Flow

## 1. 🔄 Unnecessary React Re-Renders

### Finding 1: Massive monolithic component — `Index.tsx` (1,655 lines 😱)

**Location**: `v3/goal_ui/src/pages/Index.tsx` (lines 128–1653)

The entire GOAP research workflow page is a single component with **20+ `useState` hooks** and **3 `useEffect` hooks**. Every state update — even a trivial one like changing `visibleSteps` — re-renders the whole component tree including the hero section, all step cards, stats grid, and modals.

```tsx
// Every tiny state update triggers 1,600+ lines of re-render
const [visibleSteps, setVisibleSteps] = useState<number>(1);
const [showFinalAnalysis, setShowFinalAnalysis] = useState(false);
```

**Fix**: Split into smaller composable sections — HeroSection, ResearchSteps, StatsPanel, FinalReport — each with focused state:

```tsx
// BEFORE: Everything in one component
const Index = () => {
  const [widgetConfig, setWidgetConfig] = useState<WidgetConfig>({...});
  const [userGoal, setUserGoal] = useState("");
  const [visibleSteps, setVisibleSteps] = useState(1);
  // ... 20 more state variables

  return (
    <div>
      <HeroSection config={widgetConfig} />          {/* re-renders on step change */}
      <GoalInput onSubmit={handleGoalSubmit} />       {/* re-renders on stats change */}
      <ResearchSteps steps={steps} visibleSteps={visibleSteps} />
      <StatsPanel steps={steps} widgetConfig={widgetConfig} />
    </div>
  );
};

// AFTER: Split into focused components
const HeroSection = React.memo(({ config }: { config: WidgetConfig }) => (
  <div>...</div>
));

const StatsPanel = React.memo(({ steps }: { steps: Step[] }) => {
  const completed = useMemo(() => 
    steps.filter(s => s.status === "completed").length, [steps]);
  // ...
});
```

### Finding 2: Inline `style` objects create new references every render

**Location**: `Index.tsx` — ~30 inline style objects (lines 910–1650)

```tsx
style={{ 
  backgroundColor: widgetConfig.backgroundColor,
  fontFamily: widgetConfig.fontFamily,
}}
style={{ borderColor: `${widgetConfig.primaryColor}40` }}
style={{ color: "#a3a3a3" }}
```

Each inline style creates a new `Object` reference on every render. Combined with no `React.memo` on children, this defeats any attempted memoization.

**Fix**: Extract static inline styles or use a style object outside the component:

```tsx
// OUTSIDE component (static — created once)
const MUTED_TEXT_STYLE = { color: "#a3a3a3" };  // created once at module level
const LIGHT_TEXT_STYLE = { color: "#f5f5f5" };

// Or for dynamic styles, use useMemo with the changing values
const heroStyle = useMemo(() => ({
  backgroundColor: widgetConfig.backgroundColor,
  fontFamily: widgetConfig.fontFamily,
}), [widgetConfig.backgroundColor, widgetConfig.fontFamily]);
```

### Finding 3: Missing `React.memo` on frequently-rendered children

**Location**: `Index.tsx` lines 1158–1176, `Agents.tsx` lines 734–752

`AgentStep` is rendered in a `.map()` loop and receives props that change individually, but it's not wrapped in `React.memo`. When `visibleSteps` increments by 1, ALL visible `AgentStep` components re-render.

```tsx
// BEFORE — every step re-renders when one step's status changes
{steps.slice(0, visibleSteps).map((step, index) => (
  <AgentStep
    title={step.title}
    status={step.status}     // <-- only this changes per step
    icon={step.icon}
    data={step.data}
    // ... 12 more props
  />
))}
```

**Fix**:

```tsx
// AgentStep.tsx
export const AgentStep = React.memo(({ title, status, ...props }: AgentStepProps) => {
  // ... component body
}, (prev, next) => {
  // Custom comparator — only re-render when visible state changes
  return prev.status === next.status 
    && prev.title === next.title 
    && prev.data === next.data;
});
```

### Finding 4: Function recreations causing child re-renders

**Location**: `Index.tsx` lines 512–596, `Agents.tsx` lines 91–110

`handleGoalSubmit`, `executeResearch`, `resetAll`, and `handleReviseSubmit` are all defined as component-internal functions, creating new function references every render. These are passed as props to child components:

```tsx
<GoalInput onSubmit={handleGoalSubmit} />              {/* new fn every render */}
<ReviseResearchForm onSubmit={handleReviseSubmit} />   {/* new fn every render */}
```

**Fix**: Wrap in `useCallback`:

```tsx
const handleGoalSubmit = useCallback(async (goal: string) => {
  // ... existing logic
}, [researchConfig, widgetConfig.enableAI, toast]);  // declare explicit deps

const resetAll = useCallback(() => {
  setUserGoal("");
  setPlanGenerated(false);
  // ...
}, []);  // stable if all setState calls use functional form
```

### Finding 5: `Agents.tsx` — 1,347-line monolith with 6 `useState` hooks

**Location**: `v3/goal_ui/src/pages/Agents.tsx` (full file)

Same problem as `Index.tsx`. The entire agent swarm page is one component with all state and render logic together. The `handleGeneratePlan`, `handleApproveResearch`, `handleStartSwarm` functions each register **6 separate `setTimeout` calls** that cascade over 40 seconds.

---

## 2. 🧠 Redundant Computations

### Finding 6: Triple array scan in JSX

**Location**: `Index.tsx` lines 1193–1221

```tsx
{steps.filter((s) => s.status === "completed").length}  {/* scan #1 */}
{steps.filter((s) => s.status === "active").length}      {/* scan #2 */}
{steps.filter((s) => s.status === "pending").length}     {/* scan #3 */}
```

Each `.filter()` iterates the **entire** `steps` array independently — O(3n) when O(n) would do.

**Fix**:

```tsx
// Compute once, render three times
const stepCounts = useMemo(() => {
  let completed = 0, active = 0, pending = 0;
  for (const s of steps) {
    if (s.status === "completed") completed++;
    else if (s.status === "active") active++;
    else pending++;
  }
  return { completed, active, pending };
}, [steps]);

// In JSX:
<div className="text-2xl font-semibold">{stepCounts.completed}</div>
<div className="text-2xl font-semibold">{stepCounts.active}</div>
<div className="text-2xl font-semibold">{stepCounts.pending}</div>
```

### Finding 7: Inline reducer computation in JSX

**Location**: `Index.tsx` line 1268

```tsx
{steps.reduce((acc, step) => acc + (step.data?.length || 0), 0)}
```

This runs on every render. For 7 steps with ~3 items each it's cheap, but if the steps array grows, this compounds.

**Fix**: Memoize with the step count:

```tsx
const totalDataPoints = useMemo(() => 
  steps.reduce((acc, step) => acc + (step.data?.length || 0), 0), 
[steps]);
```

### Finding 8: Date objects created inline in `Agents.tsx` render

**Location**: `Agents.tsx` lines 995–1012

```tsx
{researchPhases.flatMap((phase, phaseIdx) => [
  <div key={`...`}>
    [{new Date(Date.now() - (5 - phaseIdx) * 8000).toLocaleTimeString()}] ▶ Starting {phase.title}
  </div>,
  ...phase.data.map((item, itemIdx) => (
    <div key={`...`}>
      [{new Date(Date.now() - (5 - phaseIdx) * 8000 + itemIdx * 1000).toLocaleTimeString()}] • {item.text}
    </div>
  )),
  // ... more Date creations
])}
```

On every render, **~25-30 `new Date()` instances** are created with `toLocaleTimeString()` calls. These are deterministic relative offsets but computed fresh each time.

**Fix**: Compute timestamps once outside the component:

```tsx
// Compute once at component level or use useMemo
const logTimestamps = useMemo(() => 
  researchPhases.map((_, phaseIdx) => ({
    start: new Date(Date.now() - (5 - phaseIdx) * 8000).toLocaleTimeString(),
    end: new Date(Date.now() - (5 - phaseIdx - 1) * 8000).toLocaleTimeString(),
  })), 
[]);  // empty deps — timestamps are fake anyway
```

### Finding 9: AJV Schema Compilation on every validation

**Location**: `v3/mcp/tool-registry.ts` lines 351–370

```typescript
private validateInput(input: Record<string, unknown>, schema: JSONSchema) {
  const validate = ajv.compile(schema);  // <-- NEW COMPILATION EVERY CALL
  const valid = validate(input);
```

AJV schema compilation is CPU-intensive — it generates a `new Function()` per compilation and runs the full codegen pipeline. This happens on **every** tool execution (line 391).

**Fix**: Cache compiled validators:

```typescript
// At class level
private compiledValidators = new WeakMap<JSONSchema, ValidateFunction>();

private validateInput(input: Record<string, unknown>, schema: JSONSchema) {
  let validate = this.compiledValidators.get(schema);
  if (!validate) {
    validate = ajv.compile(schema);
    this.compiledValidators.set(schema, validate);
  }
  const valid = validate(input);
  // ...
}
```

---

## 3. 💾 Memory Leaks

### Finding 10: `AgentStep` — timeouts never cleaned up on unmount or status change

**Location**: `v3/goal_ui/src/components/AgentStep.tsx` lines 66–93

```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  if (status === "active" && data.length > 0) {
    data.forEach((_, idx) => {
      setTimeout(() => {                    // 🚫 never cleared
        setLoadingItems(prev => new Set([...prev, idx]));
        setTimeout(() => {                  // 🚫 nested — also never cleared
          setLoadingItems(prev => { ... });
          setCompletedItems(prev => new Set([...prev, idx]));
        }, 800);
      }, idx * 150);
    });
  }
}, [status, data.length]);  // <-- re-runs when status or data.length changes
```

**Why it leaks**: 
1. When `status` changes from `"active"` to `"completed"` or component unmounts, pending `setTimeout` callbacks still fire and call `setLoadingItems`/`setCompletedItems` — **React state update on unmounted component**
2. If `data.length` changes mid-animation, OLD timeouts complete and set state that overwrites NEW state
3. With 3 items per step, this leaks up to 9 timer references per transition

**Fix**:

```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  if (status !== "active" || data.length === 0) return;

  const timers: NodeJS.Timeout[] = [];

  data.forEach((_, idx) => {
    const t1 = setTimeout(() => {
      setLoadingItems(prev => new Set([...prev, idx]));
      const t2 = setTimeout(() => {
        setLoadingItems(prev => { const next = new Set(prev); next.delete(idx); return next; });
        setCompletedItems(prev => new Set([...prev, idx]));
      }, 800);
      timers.push(t2);  // track nested timer
    }, idx * 150);
    timers.push(t1);    // track outer timer
  });

  return () => {
    timers.forEach(clearTimeout);  // 🎯 cleanup on unmount or dep change
  };
}, [status, data.length]);
```

### Finding 11: `Index.tsx` — setTimeout in effects without cleanup

**Location**: `Index.tsx` lines 878–906

```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  if (goapCardsRef.current && showGOAPCards) {
    setTimeout(() => {                    // 🚫 Timer leak
      goapCardsRef.current?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
    }, 200);

    setTimeout(() => {                    // 🚫 Timer leak
      objectiveRef.current?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'center' });
    }, 4200);
  }
}, [showGOAPCards]);

useEffect(() => {
  if (finalAnalysisRef.current && showFinalAnalysis) {
    setTimeout(() => { ... }, 300);       // 🚫 Timer leak
  }
}, [showFinalAnalysis]);
```

**Fix**:

```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  if (!showGOAPCards) return;
  const t1 = setTimeout(() => goapCardsRef.current?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' }), 200);
  const t2 = setTimeout(() => objectiveRef.current?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'center' }), 4200);
  return () => { clearTimeout(t1); clearTimeout(t2); };
}, [showGOAPCards]);
```

### Finding 12: `executeResearch()` — accumulated timer leaks during step execution

**Location**: `Index.tsx` lines 599–815

The `executeResearch()` function is an async function that uses `await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, N))` pattern. If the user navigates away or clicks "Reset" mid-execution, there is no `AbortController` or cancellation mechanism:

```typescript
// No cancellation possible — runs to completion even if user hits "New Research"
const executeResearch = async () => {
  // ...
  for (let i = 0; i < workingSteps.length; i++) {
    await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 2000));  // forced delay
    // still mutates state...
    setSteps((prev) => {
      const newSteps = [...prev];
      newSteps[i].status = "completed";
      return newSteps;
    });
  }
};
```

**Fix**: Add an abort controller pattern:

```typescript
const executeResearch = async (stepsToExecute?: Step[], researchGoal?: string) => {
  const abortController = new AbortController();
  // Store in a ref for external cancellation
  abortRef.current = abortController;

  for (let i = 0; i < workingSteps.length; i++) {
    if (abortController.signal.aborted) break;

    await sleep(2000, abortController.signal);  // abortable sleep

    if (abortController.signal.aborted) break;
    setSteps(prev => { ... });
  }
};

// Helper
function sleep(ms: number, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<void> {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal?.aborted) return resolve();
    const timer = setTimeout(resolve, ms);
    signal?.addEventListener('abort', () => { clearTimeout(timer); resolve(); });
  });
}

// In resetAll:
const resetAll = useCallback(() => {
  abortRef.current?.abort();  // 🎯 cancel in-flight execution
  // ... reset state
}, []);
```

### Finding 13: `useEffect` debug logging in production

**Location**: `Agents.tsx` lines 216–219

```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("Workflow stage changed to:", workflowStage);
  console.log("Dev phase:", devPhase);
}, [workflowStage, devPhase]);
```

This runs on EVERY mount and state change — adding closure overhead and console allocations that accumulate in browser memory.

**Fix**: Remove or gate behind `process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development'`.

---

## 4. ⚡ Caching Opportunities

### Finding 14: `QueryClient` created without cache configuration

**Location**: `v3/goal_ui/src/App.tsx` line 11

```typescript
const queryClient = new QueryClient();  // default staleTime: 0
```

TanStack Query default `staleTime: 0` means every component mount triggers a background refetch. With no query mutations in this app, every fetch result is immediately considered stale.

**Fix**:

```typescript
const queryClient = new QueryClient({
  defaultOptions: {
    queries: {
      staleTime: 30_000,    // 30s — data fresh for 30 seconds
      gcTime: 5 * 60_000,   // 5 min — keep in cache
      refetchOnWindowFocus: false,  // only if app benefits from it
      retry: 2,
    },
  },
});
```

### Finding 15: No batch processing in step-to-step context passing

**Location**: `Index.tsx` lines 657–690, 777–790

The `executeResearch()` builds a progressively larger context object every step:

```typescript
// Step N: copies steps 0..N, maps all their data — O(n²) accumulation
const previousStepsData = workingSteps.slice(0, i).map(step => ({
  stepTitle: step.title,
  data: step.data.map(item => ({ ... }))  // nested map
}));
```

By step 6, this is mapping 6 steps × 3 items each = 18 object transformations **every time the step context is built** — and it's rebuilt for each new step.

**Fix**: Accumulate incrementally:

```typescript
const contextAccumulatorRef = useRef<any[]>([]);

// Append only the NEW step's data each iteration
contextAccumulatorRef.current.push({
  stepTitle: currentStep.title,
  data: currentStep.data.map(item => ({ ... })),
});
// Use contextAccumulatorRef.current directly instead of rebuilding
```

### Finding 16: `getStats()` and `getSessionMetrics()` iterate sessions independently

**Location**: `v3/mcp/session-manager.ts` lines 268–339

Two separate methods iterate the same `sessions` Map independently:

```typescript
getSessionMetrics(): SessionMetrics {      // iterates all sessions
  for (const session of this.sessions.values()) { ... }
}

getStats(): { ... } {                       // iterates all sessions AGAIN
  for (const session of this.sessions.values()) { ... }
}
```

When both are called (e.g., health check line 291), the Map is scanned twice.

**Fix**: Compute both in one pass or cache stats with a dirty flag:

```typescript
private statsCache: { metrics: SessionMetrics; stats: ReturnType<typeof this.getStats> } | null = null;
private statsDirty = true;

private recomputeStats(): void {
  // Single pass over sessions
  let authenticated = 0, active = 0, oldest, newest;
  const byState: Record<string, number> = {};
  // ... compute everything in one loop
  this.statsCache = { metrics: { ... }, stats: { ... } };
  this.statsDirty = false;
}

markDirty(): void { this.statsDirty = true; }  // call on session create/close
```

### Finding 17: Tool execution result is always JSON.stringify'd

**Location**: `v3/mcp/tool-registry.ts` line 428

```typescript
return {
  content: [{
    type: 'text',
    text: typeof result === 'string' ? result : JSON.stringify(result, null, 2),  // 🐌 pretty-print on every call
  }],
};
```

The `null, 2` indentation adds significant overhead for large tool results. Each element is space-inefficient.

**Fix**: Use compact JSON and only pretty-print in debug mode:

```typescript
text: typeof result === 'string' ? result : JSON.stringify(result)
// Remove null, 2 — saves ~30-50% response size for nested objects
```

---

## 5. 🛠️ N+1 Query Patterns (Backend)

### Finding 18: Sequential per-connection operations

**Location**: `v3/mcp/connection-pool.ts` lines 135–144

```typescript
private async initializeMinConnections(): Promise<void> {
  for (let i = 0; i < this.config.minConnections; i++) {
    await this.createConnection();  // sequential — each waits for previous
  }
}
```

Each connection creation awaits the previous one. With `minConnections: 2`, this is minor, but if scaled to 10+, it adds latency.

**Fix** (already partially done — note the `promises` array):

```typescript
// Currently has unused promises array — should Promise.all instead
private async initializeMinConnections(): Promise<void> {
  const promises: Promise<void>[] = [];         // declared but not used!
  for (let i = 0; i < this.config.minConnections; i++) {
    promises.push(this.createConnection());      // push but never await-all'd
  }
  // Missing: await Promise.all(promises);
}
```

Wait — looking more carefully, the code creates an array `promises` but only `await`s each individually inside the loop. The array is allocated but never used with `Promise.all`. This is dead allocation + sequential execution when parallel was intended.

---

## 6. 🏗️ Architectural Issues

### Finding 19: `DEFAULT_CONFIG` object spread in constructor hot path

**Location**: `v3/mcp/server.ts` line 131

```typescript
this.config = { ...DEFAULT_CONFIG, ...config } as MCPServerConfig;
```

This happens on every server instantiation. For a singleton server this is fine, but if the constructor is called many times in tests, the spread allocates a new config object each time.

### Finding 20: `setupEventHandlers()` registers 4 `on` listeners that emit the same event names

**Location**: `v3/mcp/server.ts` lines 749–779

The server listens to `tool:registered`, `tool:called`, `tool:completed`, `tool:error` from the registry and re-emits them as its own events. This is an N-to-N event forwarding pattern — every registry event creates a server event, doubling the event emission overhead.

**Fix**: If consumers don't need server-scoped events, forward selectively or have them listen to the registry directly.

